      "stdout": "schema: crypto.autoresearch.runtime_session_receipt.v2\nextends: crypto.autoresearch.runtime_session_receipt.v1\nschema_id: runtime_session_receipt.v2\nadditive_note: >-\n  This schema is a superset of crypto.autoresearch.runtime_session_receipt.v1.\n  It adds three authoritative host_binding fields (host_id, filesystem_id,\n  filesystem_mount) with declared provenance, verification semantics, and\n  failure behavior, consistent with the authoritative-binding rule in\n  coordination/goals/GOAL-ENDO-001/batches/BATCH-90392b/tasks/TASK-20260825-74615f/j3-closure-v5.yaml\n  (operation_schema_v2.host_binding). It does not edit, replace, or\n  reinterpret the v5 closure file or the v1 schema; both remain the frozen,\n  immutable base. Where this schema restates a v5 value (e.g. normalization\n  rules) it is a literal copy for closure purposes, not a correction.\n\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# v1 backward compatibility\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------\nv1_backward_compatibility:\n  statement: >-\n    A receipt carrying schema: crypto.autoresearch.runtime_session_receipt.v1\n    is parseable under v2 without modification. The v1 host_binding block\n    (which carries only a `note` field recording the absence of authoritative\n    binding fields) is accepted by the v2 parser as a structurally valid\n    host_binding object. No v1 field is removed, renamed, or reinterpreted.\n  v1_receipts_in_corpus:\n    - coordination/goals/GOAL-ENDO-001/batches/BATCH-90392b/reviews/TASK-20260825-0d566b/runtime-session-receipt.json\n    - coordination/goals/GOAL-ENDO-001/batches/BATCH-90392b/reviews/TASK-20260825-306cc8/runtime-session-receipt.json\n  v1_receipt_host_binding_shape: >-\n    { \"host_binding\": { \"note\": \"<absence note string>\" } }\n  v2_receipt_host_binding_shape: >-\n    { \"host_binding\": { \"host_id\": \"<string>\", \"filesystem_id\": \"<string>\",\n    \"filesystem_mount\": \"<string>\" } }\n\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# NAMED WEAKENING: where v1 compatibility weakens a v5 STOP\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------\nv1_compatibility_weakening_of_v5_stop:\n  named_here: true\n  v5_rule_text_evaluated: >-\n    j3-closure-v5.yaml operation_schema_v2.host_binding:\n      missing_receipt_field: STOP\n      receipt_field_absent_handling: \"If runtime-session-receipt.json lacks\n      host_binding.host_id, host_binding.filesystem_id, or\n      host_binding.filesystem_mount, the validity check itself is unevaluable\n      and STOPs; no fallback host source is substituted.\"\n  weakening: >-\n    The v2 parser accepts a v1 receipt (host_binding with only a `note` field,\n    no host_id/filesystem_id/filesystem_mount) as SCHEMA-VALID. The v5 rule\n    demands STOP when those fields are absent. Therefore: v2 schema-validity\n    does NOT imply v5 binding-rule satisfaction. A consumer that treats\n    \"parses under v2\" as \"binding is present and authoritative\" silently\n    weakens the v5 missing_receipt_field STOP. This weakening is NAMED here,\n    not hidden. The v5 STOP is preserved at the BINDING-RULE layer\n    (operation_schema_v2.host_binding.missing_receipt_field), not at the\n    schema-parser layer. The schema parser and the binding rule are separate\n    evaluation steps; the binding rule MUST be evaluated after parsing and\n    MUST STOP on absent fields regardless of schema-validity.\n  mitigation: >-\n    Any consumer (reviewer, dispatcher, or validity check) that reads a v2\n    receipt MUST evaluate the v5 binding_rule_v2 and missing_receipt_field\n    rule after parsing. A v1-shaped receipt that parses under v2 but lacks\n    the three authoritative fields MUST still STOP under the binding rule.\n    Schema-validity is necessary but not sufficient for binding-rule\n    satisfaction.\n\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Authoritative host_binding fields (v2 additions)\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------\nhost_binding:\n  schema_id: authoritative_host_binding.v2\n  source: >-\n    The active execution binding used by host_observation.validity.binding_rule\n    is read exclusively from this task's own immutable\n    runtime-session-receipt.json fields host_binding.host_id,\n    host_binding.filesystem_id, and host_binding.filesystem_mount, emitted\n    by the runtime harness at session start via emit_host_binding.sh, which\n    reads the host's own identity surfaces and never accepts caller input.\n  caller_input_is_never_authoritative: true\n  forbidden_source: host_observation object supplied by the caller/consumer\n  normalization:\n    filesystem_id: lowercase, trimmed, no trailing slash\n    filesystem_mount: absolute POSIX path, trailing slash stripped except root \"/\"\n    host_id: trimmed, case-preserved\n  binding_rule_v2: >-\n    host_observation.host_id, host_observation.filesystem_id, and\n    host_observation.filesystem_mount (each normalized as above) must be\n    exactly equal to the corresponding normalized\n    runtime-session-receipt.json host_binding.* fields. Equality against\n    any other source (including a caller-declared \"active binding\" field)\n    is rejected.\n  missing_receipt_field: STOP\n  receipt_field_absent_handling: >-\n    If runtime-session-receipt.json lacks host_binding.host_id,\n    host_binding.filesystem_id, or host_binding.filesystem_mount, the\n    validity check itself is unevaluable and STOPs; no fallback host\n    source is substituted.\n\n  fields:\n    host_id:\n      provenance: >-\n        The machine's hostname surface. On macOS this is the value returned\n        by `hostname` (the system hostname, equivalent to the HostName /\n        LocalHostName / ComputerName surface exposed by scutil). On Linux\n        this is the value returned by `hostname` (from hostname(1), or\n        /proc/sys/kernel/hostname). The value is sourced from the host's\n        own identity surface at the moment emit_host_binding.sh runs; it is\n        never read from caller input, environment variables the caller\n        controls, or repository state.\n      verification_semantics: >-\n        A reviewer or dispatcher independently checks this value by running\n        `hostname` on the same host and comparing the trimmed, case-preserved\n        result to the receipt's host_binding.host_id. The verification\n        command is: `hostname`. Agreement means the receipt captured the\n        host's hostname surface; disagreement means the receipt value does\n        not match the host (either the host changed, or the emitting session\n        misreported it). This is an independent cross-check of the CAPTURED\n        value, not of the emitting session's honesty (see\n        what_this_does_not_attest below).\n      failure_behavior: >-\n        If `hostname` exits nonzero or returns an empty string after\n        trimming, emit_host_binding.sh writes a diagnostic to stderr, emits\n        NO partial JSON block on stdout, and exits nonzero. No placeholder\n        value is substituted. The receipt is not emitted, so the v5\n        missing_receipt_field STOP applies by absence.\n\n    filesystem_id:\n      provenance: >-\n        The filesystem device identifier of the working directory the\n        procedure runs in. On macOS this is the first column of `df -P .`\n        (the device path, e.g. /dev/disk5s1). On Linux this is the first\n        column of `df -P .` or `findmnt -no SOURCE .`. The value identifies\n        the block device backing the filesystem on which the session's\n        working directory resides. It is sourced from the host's own\n        filesystem identity surface at the moment emit_host_binding.sh runs;\n        it is never read from caller input.\n      verification_semantics: >-\n        A reviewer or dispatcher independently checks this value by running\n        `df -P .` on the same host (from the same working directory) and\n        comparing the first column, normalized to lowercase / trimmed / no\n        trailing slash, to the receipt's host_binding.filesystem_id. The\n        verification command is: `df -P . | tail -1 | awk '{print $1}'`\n        followed by the v5 normalization (lowercase, trim, strip trailing\n        slash). Agreement means the receipt captured the working directory's\n        backing device; disagreement means the receipt value does not match\n        the host's current filesystem mapping (the directory was moved, the\n        device was remounted, or the emitting session misreported it).\n      failure_behavior: >-\n        If `df -P .` exits nonzero, returns no data line, or the first\n        column is empty after normalization, emit_host_binding.sh writes a\n        diagnostic to stderr, emits NO partial JSON block on stdout, and\n        exits nonzero. No placeholder value is substituted.\n\n    filesystem_mount:\n      provenance: >-\n        The mount point of the filesystem on which the working directory\n        resides. On macOS this is the trailing field(s) of `df -P .` (the\n        \"Mounted on\" column, e.g. /Volumes/SSD990). On Linux this is the\n        trailing field(s) of `df -P .` or `findmnt -no TARGET .`. The value\n        is the absolute POSIX path at which the backing device is mounted.\n        It is sourced from the host's own filesystem identity surface at the\n        moment emit_host_binding.sh runs; it is never read from caller input.\n      verification_semantics: >-\n        A reviewer or dispatcher independently checks this value by running\n        `df -P .` on the same host (from the same working directory) and\n        comparing the mount-point column, normalized to absolute POSIX path\n        with trailing slash stripped except root \"/\", to the receipt's\n        host_binding.filesystem_mount. The verification command is:\n        `df -P . | tail -1 | sed -E 's/^([^[:space:]]+[[:space:]]+){5}//'`\n        followed by the v5 normalization. Agreement means the receipt\n        captured the working directory's mount point; disagreement means the\n        receipt value does not match the host's current mount table.\n      failure_behavior: >-\n        If `df -P .` exits nonzero, returns no data line, or the mount-point\n        field is empty after normalization, emit_host_binding.sh writes a\n        diagnostic to stderr, emits NO partial JSON block on stdout, and\n        exits nonzero. No placeholder value is substituted.\n\n  what_this_does_not_attest: >-\n    The host_binding fields attest that the emitting session's\n    emit_host_binding.sh captured the host's identity surfaces at emission\n    time. They do NOT attest that the emitting session is honest: a\n    compromised emitting session can still hand-write a receipt with\n    arbitrary host_binding values, or run the script on a different host\n    and paste the output. The v5 closure's threat model assumed\n    caller-supplied bindings were the attack; this v2 extension narrows\n    that gap (a caller can no longer supply arbitrary values THROUGH the\n    schema or the emit procedure) but does not fully close it (a\n    compromised emitting session can still misreport its host). Independent\n    verification by a reviewer or dispatcher (running the verification\n    commands on the same host) is the cross-check that detects a\n    misreported binding; it is not automatic and must be performed\n    deliberately.\n\n  limitation_disclosed: true\n  limitation: >-\n    LIMITATION (required disclosure, carried forward from the v5 closure and\n    narrowed, not closed): the v2 schema and emit_host_binding.sh move the\n    authoritative host binding from a SPECIFIABLE RULE to an EMITTED RULE\n    with a runnable procedure and declared verification semantics. The gap\n    narrows: a caller can no longer supply arbitrary host_binding values\n    through the schema (the emit procedure reads the host's own surfaces,\n    and the v5 binding_rule_v2 rejects equality against any non-receipt\n    source). The gap does not fully close: the emitting session's honesty\n    is assumed by the v5 threat model, and nothing in this package\n    INDEPENDENTLY cross-checks the capture at emission time. Independent\n    verification (a reviewer running the verification commands on the same\n    host) detects a misreported binding after the fact but is not enforced\n    automatically. This is the residual self-attestation gap. It is\n    disclosed here rather than hidden, and it is the expected scope of\n    what this batch closes: HOST-BINDING-ENFORCEABILITY is narrowed from\n    \"specifiable rule, no harness\" to \"emitted rule with runnable harness\n    and declared verification, residual self-attestation gap disclosed,\"\n    against the v5 threat model (caller-supplied bindings), not against a\n    malicious-runtime threat model.\n",
